WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:03:14.618 --> 00:03:18.589
Graduates, guests, faculty and staff.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.360
Welcome to the 2021 MSX
graduation ceremony at

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.504
the Stanford University Graduate School
of Business.

00:03:29.504 --> 00:03:34.064
11 months ago,
you arrived here at the GSB and

00:03:34.064 --> 00:03:37.260
I welcomed your class over Zoom.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.910
I am so
happy that we are here together in SEMX.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:48.290
We have definitely come a long
way over the past year.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.122
Now, before we begin the ceremony,

00:03:52.122 --> 00:03:55.974
I'd like to start by recognizing
our guests, the families and

00:03:55.974 --> 00:04:00.720
friends who have provided support and
made the effort to be here today.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.639
Let's have a round of applause for
our guests.

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:11.220
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.734
I'd also like to share a brief video
message of congratulations from

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:17.110
Stanford's President Mirth Cassia Vivian.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.865
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon, and

00:04:21.865 --> 00:04:26.190
congratulations to each of you on earning
your Masters of Science in Management.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.741
During your time at Stanford,
you've explored intellectually,

00:04:30.741 --> 00:04:34.730
we find your skills and reflected on your
careers and your goals for the future.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:38.330
I'm always impressed
with our MSX students for

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.812
taking the time to invest in themselves
that advance their knowledge.

00:04:41.812 --> 00:04:46.330
But your cohort in particular took on
this task at a deeply uncertain time.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:52.242
You persevere through a delayed start,
adapted to new ways of learning, and

00:04:52.242 --> 00:04:56.199
built a strong community,
despite the challenges you faced.

00:04:56.199 --> 00:04:59.653
The spirit of leadership determination and
commitment that you honed

00:04:59.653 --> 00:05:04.180
through this experience will help you
measurably throughout your careers.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.848
Your hard work and
dedication are brought to you today,

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:08.960
and I hope you're all tremendously proud.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:14.392
As you join the ranks of Stanford alumni,
I urge you to use your talents and

00:05:14.392 --> 00:05:17.882
the skills you've honed here to
shape the lives you want to lead and

00:05:17.882 --> 00:05:19.839
to contribute to our broader world.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.856
I can't wait to see what you do next,

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:29.239
congratulations once again,
on your remarkable achievements.

00:05:29.239 --> 00:05:37.974
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; A graduation

00:05:37.974 --> 00:05:44.120
ceremony is about celebrating what you've
achieved and what you will go on to do.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.418
And I'd like to start by looking back and

00:05:47.418 --> 00:05:52.561
let's start by rolling back
the clock to the fall of 2019.

00:05:52.561 --> 00:05:56.056
You're all leading your lives
very successful lives by

00:05:56.056 --> 00:06:00.514
all accounts spread out all over
the globe in more than 20 countries,

00:06:00.514 --> 00:06:06.035
working in different industries finance,
healthcare, tech education government.

00:06:06.035 --> 00:06:10.937
Many of you had advanced degrees
already many of you had children

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.578
spouses mortgages.

00:06:12.578 --> 00:06:16.736
You were leading teams,
the mortgage part yeah,

00:06:16.736 --> 00:06:22.184
you were leading teams,
some of you had started organizations.

00:06:22.184 --> 00:06:27.997
And yet you wanted something more,
you wanted to think about your career,

00:06:27.997 --> 00:06:33.547
your life, how you might take it in
a new direction or to the next level.

00:06:33.547 --> 00:06:38.189
You are dreaming bigger, and
that led you to Stanford, or

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:41.900
you thought that was
leading you to Stanford.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.200
Of course, March 2020 rolled around and
had other plans for all of us.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.739
And the GSB, like the rest of the world,

00:06:50.739 --> 00:06:55.460
had to move online, and your path here
became much less straightforward.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:01.923
We made a difficult decision to postpone
the start date of your program,

00:07:01.923 --> 00:07:05.227
making you unique in
the history of the GSB.

00:07:05.227 --> 00:07:08.370
And of course you had
many factors to consider.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:13.160
Your jobs, your family, visas,
housing, expiring, GMAT scores.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.748
Not to mention maintaining your own
physical and mental at the onset and

00:07:18.748 --> 00:07:21.444
that of your family at
the onset of the pandemic.

00:07:21.444 --> 00:07:25.958
And you had to answer a basic question,
would it make sense to put

00:07:25.958 --> 00:07:30.889
everything on hold and come to Stanford
for a year to study business and

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:34.750
to study leadership in
the midst of a global pandemic?

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:40.135
Well, you're here so
clearly the answer was yes.

00:07:41.386 --> 00:07:44.011
And Martin Luther King, Jr. once said,

00:07:44.011 --> 00:07:48.440
you don't have to see the whole staircase,
just take the first step.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.645
You took that step, and you didn't know
how the program was going to go or

00:07:54.645 --> 00:07:58.815
whether we would be able to resume in
person learning whether you would ever

00:07:58.815 --> 00:08:03.570
meet The faculty members in person or
your classmates for that matter.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:09.200
You took that step and you took a risk.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.420
And that says a lot about you,
individually and collectively.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.461
And over the last year you move from
interacting with each other over Zoom to

00:08:18.461 --> 00:08:23.500
sharing classrooms with your cohort to the
Super Summer and now to the full quarter.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.890
And you came together as a class.

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:29.230
You planned virtual kids events,
you held MSX moments,

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.270
conversations with professors and
other speakers at EVGR.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.357
You've been drove around apparently
in a pickup truck helping your other

00:08:36.357 --> 00:08:37.160
classmates move.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:41.910
You took paths to power with Jeff Pfeffer.

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:46.162
David Krebs pushed your
knowledge of micro economics,

00:08:46.162 --> 00:08:51.155
Jennifer Aaker enlightened you about the
relationship between business and humor.

00:08:51.155 --> 00:08:53.409
You refined your leadership skills and

00:08:53.409 --> 00:08:56.896
interpersonal dynamics in
managing growing enterprises.

00:08:56.896 --> 00:08:59.400
You earned the admiration of our faculty.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:08:59.976
In fact,

00:08:59.976 --> 00:09:05.026
some of our faculty are now advocating to
teach their classes in MSX only elective.

00:09:05.026 --> 00:09:09.945
[APPLAUSE] You've taken
the time to think about

00:09:09.945 --> 00:09:15.241
what you want to do,
you want to change your career,

00:09:15.241 --> 00:09:21.350
maybe start a company with
someone you've met here.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:25.985
Or maybe you've realized that you want to
return to your job, to your industry but

00:09:25.985 --> 00:09:27.426
with a new perspective.

00:09:27.426 --> 00:09:31.876
The magic of being at Stanford is being
exposed to a breadth of people of ideas

00:09:31.876 --> 00:09:35.780
of skills of different approaches
to management and leadership.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.150
One thing is certain.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:42.030
There will be many opportunities for
each of you in the years to come.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:46.635
And I hope that when you remember this
year, you will remember it both for

00:09:46.635 --> 00:09:51.480
the opportunities it created and
for the people that you met.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.810
And for the chance that you had to reset
and reflect on your own aspirations,

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:02.069
about how you wanted to pursue the next
phase of your career and your lives.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.612
And I hope you will do that in ways
that are filled with meaning and

00:10:06.612 --> 00:10:08.450
contribute to the greater good.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.643
You are graduating at a time of enormous
change and acceleration of technology,

00:10:15.643 --> 00:10:20.959
a rethinking of work the opportunity
to build and reshape organizations.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:27.253
And with the skills and the knowledge
that will help you go on in business to

00:10:27.253 --> 00:10:31.200
tackle some of the great challenges and
changes of our time.

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:33.845
A time of disruption and

00:10:33.845 --> 00:10:38.919
change is exactly the moment when
a Stanford education is the most valuable.

00:10:38.919 --> 00:10:42.989
And more than other classes
perhaps more than any other class.

00:10:42.989 --> 00:10:47.086
You've learned how to deal with
uncertainty and with ambiguity,

00:10:47.086 --> 00:10:49.770
to be adaptable, to be resilient.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.720
And those are powerful lessons for
your career and your life.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:58.270
MSX class of 2021 congratulations.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.640
I wish you all the best.

00:11:03.835 --> 00:11:09.021
[APPLAUSE] And now I have the great
pleasure of introducing our

00:11:09.021 --> 00:11:15.948
graduation speaker Noosheen Hashemi,
a graduate of the MSX class of 1993.

00:11:15.948 --> 00:11:20.841
Noosheen is a tech veteran,
an entrepreneur, an investor,

00:11:20.841 --> 00:11:22.610
a philanthropist.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:27.115
She is the founder and CEO of January AI,
a precision health tech company that

00:11:27.115 --> 00:11:31.345
harnesses the power of artificial
intelligence to prevent, predict,

00:11:31.345 --> 00:11:33.650
postpone and manage chronic disease.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:36.197
Noosheen also guides a family office that

00:11:36.197 --> 00:11:39.860
